# The contacts application

`src/apps/contacts.py`, `contacts_state.py`, `contacts_web.py`,
`contacts_link.py` · page `src/webassets/contacts.py` (console `/contacts`)

The address books of a node. A **contact** is a name a person chose and the
nodes it answers to; a **book** is a named list of contacts. A node keeps as
many books as it likes (family, work, the ones other people shared), and **every
other app asks for them** — chat keeps no list of its own beyond a copy it draws
the screen from.

It is a built-in app like chat: it runs on the data connector under its own app
id, keeps its books in its own encrypted drawer (never in the clear on disk), and
is on by default. Turn it off from *Apps* and `/contacts` and `/api/contacts/*`
stop existing.

## Names are somebody's own word

What a node is called by *itself* is a signed claim the node holds
([`../Pseudos/guide`](../Pseudos/guide)). What a **contact** is called is what the
person who owns the book wrote. The two are kept apart: the page shows the node's
own name beside the one the person chose, never in place of it, and a nickname
cannot make a stranger look like somebody else (a name always travels with the
node id).

## The permission

Android's model: a person decides, per app, whether it may touch the contacts.

| Permission | Level | Allows |
|---|---|---|
| `contacts` | sensitive | everything below |
| `contacts.read` | sensitive | list the books and their contacts, search by name or node id, ask who a node is |
| `contacts.write` | sensitive | add a node as a contact, take one out |

Nothing is granted by asking. An app that asks for them says why on the
permissions page (chat: "it shows the people you know by the names you gave
them" / "it adds a person to your address book when you press *Add to
contacts*"). Reading does not let an app write, taking back `contacts` takes
back its children, and a client holding only the connector's shared token never
gets either (`Docs/AppPermissions/guide`).

The operations are declared with the permission they need
(`app_api.operation(..., permission="contacts.read")`), and the control plane
answers an **app** caller from that — so the check is made where the call is
made, not left to the app being asked:

| Operation | Needs | Changes | Answers |
|---|---|---|---|
| `books` | `contacts.read` | no | id, name, colour, count, default/writable/shared flags |
| `list` | `contacts.read` | no | the contacts of one book (or all), without their notes |
| `names` | `contacts.read` | no | every node in the books and the name its owner gave it |
| `search` | `contacts.read` | no | contacts matching a name, a label, or the start of a node id |
| `find` | `contacts.read` | no | the contacts that answer to a node |
| `add` | `contacts.write` | yes | save a node (named from the directory when no name is given) |
| `remove` | `contacts.write` | yes | take a node out of one book or all of them |

None of them is `remote`: a console managing this node reaches the books only
with its `apps` grant, and `/api/contacts/` is on the same list as
`/api/chat/` (`_NEEDS_APPS`). The note a person wrote beside a contact never
leaves through the API.

### How an app uses it

`src/apps/contacts_link.py`:

```python
link = ContactsLink(host, perms, my_app_id_hex)
link.access()                  # "write" | "read" | "denied" | "unavailable"
link.call("find", node=hex)    # Unavailable | Denied(permission) | AppAPIError
```

The host and the permission book are looked up on every call, so a contacts app
stopped a second ago is not reachable through a reference the link kept. A refused
app gets a **refusal** (`Denied` naming the permission), never a thinner answer —
so it can ask the person instead of showing an empty list that looks true.

## Finding somebody on a network of thousands

Search is two questions asked in order: the books first (instant, nothing leaves
the node), then the directory (`wide`), which is the node's own ranked name
search and asks the network for an exact name. Saved contacts come first in
chat's search (`match = -1`), found by the name their owner gave them as well as
the one the node signs. Ranking is `pseudo.fold` + `rank_folded` — the same
rules as the directory, so a name found here and one found there sort alike.

## The data

`ContactsState` is the data and its rules, no sockets. Every collection is hard
bounded:

| Limit | Value |
|---|---|
| books | 32 |
| contacts per book (tombstones included) | 1000 |
| nodes per contact | 8 |
| name / label / note | 64 / 32 / 512 characters |
| members of a shared book | 32 |
| invitations waiting for a human | 16 in all, 2 per sender, 14 days |

A book is one value of the node's drawer (256 KiB at most); a book that no
longer fits is refused with `full` rather than truncated. Deleted contacts leave
a tombstone for 90 days so the deletion reaches nodes that were offline; they
are the first thing to go when room is needed.

## Sharing a book with another node

A book is shared by **invitation**, and an invitation is not trusted: a node that
offers a book costs the other a line in a queue until a human accepts. A contact
called "Bank" planted in somebody's address book is a social-engineering attack,
so the only way into a book is a person saying yes. Roles are `read` and `write`.

**The owner numbers.** The node that made the book gives changes a sequence
number; a member that may write sends its edits to the owner, which orders them
and passes them on. Each entry carries a version `[counter, writer]`, ordered by
counter and then by writer id (last write wins), so every replica settles on the
same entry without a clock anyone has to believe. If the owner is gone for good
the copies stay readable and, where writable, editable — they stop following each
other, nothing more. The owner can also let a member go (`revoke` detaches its
copy, which becomes the member's own book).

Wire format inside each E2E `DATA` payload: one type byte, then a JSON object.

| Type | Sent | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `OFFER` (1) | owner → node | "here is a book, if you want it" |
| `ACCEPT` / `DECLINE` (2, 3) | node → owner | the human's answer |
| `LEAVE` (4) | member → owner | "I no longer follow this book" |
| `PULL` (5) | member → owner | "what changed since this sequence?" |
| `PUSH` (6) | either way | contacts, newest wins (20 per page) |
| `ACK` (7) | owner → member | "I have these of yours" |
| `REVOKE` (8) | owner → member | "this book is no longer shared with you" |

Everything is validated before it is looked at: a message over 40 000 bytes, one
that is not what its type says, a push to a book that was not accepted, an edit
from a reader, an entry whose version jumps past the bound — all dropped, with
the sender reported (`AppGuard`: invitations 4/60 s, answers 20/10 s, pulls and
pushes 30 and 60/10 s). A pull for a book this node does not own is answered
with nothing (not even "no such book"), and `REVOKE` is only ever sent to a node
that really is not a member. A round runs every 300 s (pulls, the pushes that
failed, invitations not yet answered, at most 12 times).

## The page (`/contacts`)

Three panes on one grid — the **books**, the **contacts** of the open book, one
**contact** — and a single attribute, `data-view="books|list|detail"`, says which
is on screen. Wide enough (over 1000 px) shows all three; narrower shows one at a
time with a back button. The page never asks which device it is on.

- books: colour, "private / shared by you / shared with you", pending
  **invitations** with Accept / Decline;
- contacts: search within the book, favourites first, add by **name from the
  network** or by pasting a node id;
- editor: name, up to eight nodes (each with a label), note, favourite, move to
  another book, **Message** (opens `/chat#c/<node id>`);
- share dialog: invite by name or id, role, who follows, take back;
- import / export as **vCard** (`X-NMESH-NODE` carries the node ids; any other
  address book reads the rest). An import is capped at 600 000 characters of text and 1000 entries (the request carrying it at 2 MiB).

Every sentence is read from the catalogue (`t()`), and a refusal from the node
travels as a stable code (`why`: `no_book`, `read_only`, `no_contact`, `empty`,
`full`, `bad_node`, `not_owner`, `self`, `gone`) that the page turns into its own
words.

## Console routes

| Method | Path | |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/api/contacts/state` | `?book=…` → books, invitations, the book's contacts, names (sectioned like chat's feed) |
| GET | `/api/contacts/people` | `?q=…&wide=0|1` → saved contacts first, then the directory |
| GET | `/api/contacts/export` | `?book=…` → a `.vcf` download |
| POST | `/api/contacts/<op>` | `save` `delete` `move` `book` `share` `unshare` `answer` `people` `import` |

All behind the console session; the page and its assets are public, like chat's.
